WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.714
The small intestine is where digested food is absorbed into the blood. Its lining is folded into millions of tiny fingers, called villi. One of them is called a villus.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:26.506
Small food molecules, such as glucose, move into the blood by diffusion. Diffusion is movement from where there is a lot, to where there is less. Each feature of a villus makes this faster.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:38.358
Feature one: there are very many villi, and the cells on each villus have tiny folds called microvilli. So the surface area for absorption is huge.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:46.560
Feature two: the wall of the villus is only one cell thick. So glucose has only a short distance to diffuse.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:01:06.226
Feature three: each villus has a dense network of capillaries, the smallest blood vessels. Blood flows through and carries the glucose away. This keeps the glucose concentration in the blood low. So the difference in concentration between the gut and the blood stays big: we call this a steep concentration gradient.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:16.745
Feature four: in the middle is a lacteal. It is a lymph vessel. It absorbs fatty acids and glycerol, the products of fat digestion.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:36.213
Now damage. In coeliac disease, the villi are flattened. The surface area falls, so less is absorbed. Less calcium is absorbed, so calcium is taken from the bones, and they become weak. Gut worms also take nutrients, so fewer reach the blood.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:52.844
Now a real exam question. The diagram shows a structure from the small intestine: a villus. Explain how these structures increase the rate of diffusion of glucose into the blood. Three marks.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:03.535
Point one: there are many villi, with microvilli, so there is a large surface area. Point two: the wall is one cell thick, so the diffusion distance is short.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:10.885
Point three: many capillaries carry the glucose away in the blood, so a steep concentration gradient is kept.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:19.597
Here is the full answer: three features, each with its effect. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:32.086
A common mistake is to say villi have thin cell walls. Human cells have no cell wall. Say the wall of the villus is one cell thick.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:45.078
Another mistake is to list features with no effect, or to say villi digest food. Villi absorb. Give each feature, then what it does for absorption.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:03:00.158
Your turn. After an operation, a man has a much shorter small intestine. He eats normally, but he loses weight. Explain why. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:08.622
A shorter small intestine has fewer villi. So there is a smaller surface area for absorption.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:19.760
So less glucose and other food is absorbed into his blood. His cells get less energy from food, so the body uses its stores, and he loses weight.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:30.296
So, every time: pick a feature of the villus, and link it to its effect. For damage: flattened or fewer villi, less surface area, less absorbed, then the result.
